<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:70%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T4.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:129%;">Table 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.T4.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:129%;">Results of timestamp-based QA on QVHighlights and traditional QA focusing on temporal cues using TempCompass and VideoMME<span id="S4.T4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span>Best<span id="S4.T4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">/<span id="S4.T4.12.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">Second</span>).
We transform annotations from QVHighlights into questions tied to specific timestamps, each expecting a ‘Yes’ or ‘No’ answer. For VideoMME, we report results on the Temporal Perception and Temporal Reasoning subsets (no subtitles).
While VideoMME benchmarks long video understanding (up to an hour), our data of entirely short videos can help improve model performance.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
